Yes, Lojban has properties and sets, but it is not a clearcut second order =
language, since these things are treated as as objects for the most part (t=
here are variables for predicates, but no special ones for properties, for =
example).=A0 The "as" situation seems to be just another case of "under con=
ditions" for which there is at least one BAI (note, "treat him as a friend"=
 uses another BAI; this is not a generic "as" solution).=A0 Lojban is also =
intenstional, since some of its sumtis' referents are properties and the li=
ke. But it is always the referents that count, even when they are the sense=
s of something else (in which case, they may interact with the rest of the =
sentence in special ways, which are, however, not at all peculiar).=A0=20




